# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
# everest-tallest-mountain (Miya's Note). Script from miya 2026-11-11 (dated 2026-10-21); fact-checked per guideline 12-1 (see script-check.md).
SCENES = [
 ('S01', 'Three Rulers', 'ill', 'p01a', 'Is Mount Everest really the tallest mountain on Earth? Everyone learns the answer in school, but the real answer depends on where you place your ruler. Measure from sea level, and Everest wins. Measure from the true base of the mountain, where its rock begins, and another giant steps forward. Measure from the center of the planet, and it is a different mountain again.'),
 ('S02', 'Three Rulers', 'ill', 'p01b', 'Three rulers, three winners. So which one is the real champion, and are there mountains on other worlds that make all of them look small?'),
 ('S03', 'Drain the Ocean', 'ill', 'p02a', 'Imagine for a moment that the ocean waters were completely drained away. What if we measured a mountain not from the surface of the sea, but from the true physical base where it rises from the crust? Or what if we measured height from the center of our planet toward the open cosmos? Suddenly, Mount Everest is no longer alone at the top. Other silent giants step forward from the deep ocean floor and the equatorial bulge of our spinning globe.'),
 ('S04', 'Drain the Ocean', 'ill', 'p02b', "When you change the reference point, the geography of our planet transforms completely. Welcome to Miya's Note, where today we explore what truly makes a mountain the tallest in the world."),
 ('S05', 'The Roof of the World', 'ill', 'p03a', 'Let us begin in the frozen realm of the Himalayas. Mount Everest straddles the border between Nepal and China, rising to an officially recognized 8,848 meters above sea level. Near its summit, a band of limestone holds marine fossils, the remains of sea creatures that lived in a shallow ocean hundreds of millions of years ago.'),
 ('S06', 'The Roof of the World', 'ill', 'p03b', 'How did ocean floor end up on the rooftop of the world? Tens of millions of years ago, the landmass of India drifted northward and collided with Eurasia. That immense collision is still taking place right beneath our feet.'),
 ('S07', 'The Roof of the World', 'ill', 'p03c', 'As the Indian plate pushes under the Eurasian plate, the crust crumples and folds, shoving ancient seabeds into the sky. Geologists estimate that the Himalayas still rise by a few millimeters each year, countered only by the destructive forces of wind, ice, and gravity.'),
 ('S08', 'The Roof of the World', 'ill', 'p03d', 'In terms of altitude above sea level, Everest remains the undisputed king of dry land. No other summit on Earth lets a human being stand higher above the ocean surface. But here is the critical question: why did we decide that sea level should be the ultimate judge of height?'),
 ('S09', 'Why Sea Level', 'ill', 'p04a', 'Sea level feels natural because human civilization grew along the coasts. We build harbors, sail across oceans, and watch the tides come and go. When nineteenth-century surveyors mapped the great mountain ranges, the ocean surface gave every country a shared starting line, a universal zero mark. It was simple, intuitive, and remarkably convenient.'),
 ('S10', 'Skyscraper Logic', 'ill', 'p05a', 'Now look at a mountain the way an engineer would look at a skyscraper. An architect does not measure a building from a street curb across town; they measure from the foundation up to the spire. Apply that same logic to mountains, and a different contender takes the crown. We must leave the freezing snows of the Himalayas and cross thousands of kilometers of open water to the islands of Hawaii.'),
 ('S11', 'Mauna Kea', 'ill', 'p06a', 'Rising from the floor of the North Pacific Ocean is the massive volcanic giant Mauna Kea. To a traveler on a sandy Hawaiian beach, it looks like a gentle, broad-shouldered mountain rising into the tropical clouds. Its summit reaches 4,207 meters above sea level, impressive, but modest next to Everest.'),
 ('S12', 'Mauna Kea', 'ill', 'p06b', 'It even hosts astronomical observatories near its peak, because the air above it is remarkably dry and clear. But that visible summit is only the tip of an unimaginably massive volcanic structure.'),
 ('S13', 'Born From a Hotspot', 'ill', 'p07a', "How did such a colossal giant form without a collision of continents? While Everest was born from a head-on crash between two continents, Mauna Kea was born from a mantle plume, often called a volcanic hotspot. Deep within the Earth, a column of intense heat melts rock and punches through the oceanic crust like a welder's torch."),
 ('S14', 'Born From a Hotspot', 'ill', 'p07b', 'Over millions of years, fluid basaltic lava erupted onto the ocean floor and cooled in the frigid water. Layer after layer stacked up over the millennia, slowly building an enormous shield volcano.'),
 ('S15', 'An Iceberg of Stone', 'chalk', 'p08a', 'Think of Mauna Kea as an iceberg of stone. Above the waves it stands 4,207 meters. But its base rests about six thousand meters below the surface of the Pacific, so measured from the seafloor, it rises roughly 10,210 meters, well over a thousand meters taller than Everest.'),
 ('S16', 'An Iceberg of Stone', 'ill', 'p08b', 'If you were a deep-sea creature crawling along the Pacific floor and looking up, you would be gazing at a stone wall more than ten kilometers high. From this perspective, Everest is a tall spire resting on an already elevated continental plateau, while Mauna Kea is the true physical titan built up from the ocean floor.'),
 ('S17', 'The Third Ruler', 'ill', 'p09a', 'For a third ruler, we must travel to the volcanic spine of Ecuador, in South America. Here stands Mount Chimborazo, an ancient, ice-capped stratovolcano in the Western Cordillera of the Andes. By its official altitude, Chimborazo seems entirely out of the running.'),
 ('S18', 'The Third Ruler', 'ill', 'p09b', "Its summit rises to 6,263 meters above the ocean, far down the list of global summits. It is not even the highest peak in the Andes; that title belongs to Aconcagua in Argentina. Yet Chimborazo holds a unique planetary distinction. Its summit is the farthest point on the Earth's surface from the center of our planet."),
 ('S19', 'The Spinning Planet', 'ill', 'p10a', 'How can a mountain more than two thousand meters shorter than Everest reach farther into space? The secret lies in the imperfect shape of the Earth. In textbooks, our planet is a smooth, perfect blue marble. In reality, it is an oblate spheroid, slightly flattened at the poles and bulging at the middle.'),
 ('S20', 'The Spinning Planet', 'ill', 'p10b', 'Our planet spins around its axis at roughly 1,670 kilometers per hour at the equator. That rotation pushes the equatorial regions outward, so the radius at the equator is about twenty-one kilometers longer than the radius at the poles.'),
 ('S21', 'On the Bulge', 'chalk', 'p11a', 'Chimborazo sits about one and a half degrees south of the equator, nestled right on top of this planetary bulge. Everest sits much farther north, at roughly twenty-eight degrees latitude, well away from the maximum bulge. Measured from the center of the Earth, the summit of Chimborazo is approximately 6,384 kilometers away. Everest comes up short by more than two thousand meters.'),
 ('S22', 'On the Bulge', 'ill', 'p11b', "Think about that astonishing reality. On the icy, wind-whipped peak of Chimborazo, you would be farther from the center of our planet than anyone standing anywhere else on Earth's surface. Looking up into the clear equatorial night sky, you would stand on the true rooftop of Earth's geometry. In this race outward, a quiet South American volcano beats Mount Everest by about two kilometers."),
 ('S23', 'Three Winners', 'ill', 'p12a', "Isn't it remarkable how a single question changes with your frame of reference? Define height by sea level, and Everest is the champion. Measure the rise from base to peak, and Mauna Kea stands supreme. Measure the distance from the center of the Earth, and Chimborazo reigns. Nature provides no single universal scoreboard; human beings design it, and the results depend on the rules we choose."),
 ('S24', 'Down Into the Trench', 'ill', 'p13a', 'This realization forces us to look at the opposite end of the scale. What about the world hidden beneath the waves, where the planet drops into darkness? In the western Pacific, near the Mariana Islands, the seabed plunges into the Mariana Trench. At its lowest known point, the Challenger Deep, the ocean floor lies nearly eleven thousand meters below the surface.'),
 ('S25', 'Down Into the Trench', 'ill', 'p13b', 'To grasp that depth, imagine carving Mount Everest from its roots and dropping it into the Challenger Deep. The entire mountain would slip beneath the dark water, with its summit still more than two kilometers under the surface.'),
 ('S26', 'A Lopsided Planet', 'ill', 'p14a', "Why can ocean trenches plunge eleven kilometers down, while our tallest mountains rise less than nine? Part of the answer is the strength of rock and the pull of gravity. A mountain is an immense load pressing down on the Earth's crust and the slowly flowing mantle beneath it."),
 ('S27', 'A Lopsided Planet', 'ill', 'p14b', 'Geologists suspect that a mountain much taller than ten kilometers would crush the rock beneath it, forcing that rock to deform and flow like soft plastic. Trenches are different. They form where one tectonic plate dives beneath another, so they are not piled up against gravity at all.'),
 ('S28', 'Leaving Earth', 'ill', 'p15a', "This raises a thrilling question: what happens when we leave Earth entirely? To witness the ultimate mountains of the Solar System, we must turn our telescopes toward the red deserts of Mars. Mars is a smaller world, with only about thirty-eight percent of our planet's surface gravity, and it has no active plate tectonics."),
 ('S29', 'Leaving Earth', 'ill', 'p15b', 'Scientists think that without moving plates, the crust stays fixed over volcanic hotspots, letting a volcano keep growing in one place for an extraordinarily long time.'),
 ('S30', 'Olympus Mons', 'chalk', 'p16a', 'Olympus Mons is a planetary wonder that defies easy comprehension. It towers approximately twenty-one to twenty-two kilometers above the surrounding Martian plains. That is about two and a half times the height of Everest above sea level, and more than twice the base-to-peak height of Mauna Kea.'),
 ('S31', 'Olympus Mons', 'ill', 'p16b', 'If you placed Olympus Mons on North America, its circular base, roughly six hundred kilometers wide, would cover a region about the size of Arizona. Its summit caldera alone spans dozens of kilometers across.'),
 ('S32', 'Olympus Mons', 'ill', 'p16c', 'What would it feel like to stand on Olympus Mons? You might expect a jagged cliff, but because it is a shield volcano, its average slope is only a few degrees. The mountain is so broad that you would hardly realize you were climbing; the ground would simply tilt gently upward into a dusty sky.'),
 ('S33', 'Rheasilvia', 'ill', 'p17a', "Mars is not the only world with towering stone. On the asteroid Vesta, a giant impact carved a crater called Rheasilvia. NASA's Dawn spacecraft found its central peak rising roughly twenty to twenty-five kilometers above the crater floor. On a small body with a tiny fraction of Earth's gravity, rock can stand far taller than on our dense, heavy planet."),
 ('S34', 'Back Home', 'ill', 'p18a', 'Now let us return from the cold deserts of Mars and the quiet rocks of the asteroid belt to our home world. Seen from an orbit hundreds of kilometers above the atmosphere, Everest, Mauna Kea, and Chimborazo all seem to disappear. Our planet looks like a smooth, glistening blue jewel suspended in an ocean of darkness.'),
 ('S35', 'A Billiard Ball', 'ill', 'p19a', 'In fact, if you shrank the Earth to the size of a billiard ball, it would feel remarkably smooth. The difference between the highest peak on land and the deepest trench in the ocean is less than twenty kilometers. On a planet more than twelve thousand seven hundred kilometers across, that is a fraction of a single percent.'),
 ('S36', 'A Billiard Ball', 'ill', 'p19b', 'The jagged peaks that have tested human courage and claimed hundreds of lives are microscopic textures on the skin of our world.'),
 ('S37', 'Which Is Highest', 'chalk', 'p20a', 'So which mountain is truly the highest? If your definition honors the human struggle against thin air and bitter cold, the answer is Mount Everest, the ultimate benchmark of endurance on dry land. If it respects the pure scale of geology, rising from the ocean floor, then Mauna Kea is the monarch of our planet. And if it celebrates our place in the wider universe, looking outward, Chimborazo is the summit that reaches farthest into space.'),
 ('S38', 'No Wrong Answer', 'ill', 'p21a', 'None of these answers is wrong. Each represents a different ruler and a different way of looking at our home. The world does not come in rigid, pre-labeled categories, and the questions we ask determine the answers we receive. When we change the baseline, we do not change the mountain; we change our understanding of our place within the landscape.'),
 ('S39', 'Tonight', 'ill', 'p22a', 'The next time you sip your morning tea or gaze out a train window at the distant horizon, remember that the ground beneath you is part of a grand, spinning sphere. Beneath the streets, tectonic plates drift slowly over flowing rock. Far across the blue horizons, ancient volcanoes anchor themselves in the deep sea, and equatorial summits reach toward the quiet stars. The world is larger, stranger, and more wondrous than our school textbooks ever led us to believe.'),
]
